VERDICT -- nwsize
=================
THE SIZE RULE IS DEAD: nw=1's ONLY REAL WIN COMES FROM OVERRUNNING THE TIME LIMIT.

Medians over two draws at a 30 s limit:

          peak_util   nw=1          nw=2          nw=3          nw=4       nw=1 vs best multi
    P25      6.26     91,014,186    FLOOR         FLOOR         FLOOR         -97.9%
    P36      4.66     97,909,179    97,503,186    97,295,273    86,220,047    +13.6%
    P13      4.09     89,607,629    77,326,216    77,687,699    87,874,924    +15.9%
    P2       3.29     81,523,006    59,689,432    61,176,109    59,996,257    +36.6%
    P23      2.79     15,774,520    15,932,384    15,932,384    15,994,737     -1.0%
    P16      1.23      3,174,098     3,252,294     3,252,294     3,352,855     -2.4%
    P3       1.18      4,766,080     4,658,612     4,719,480     4,747,164     +2.3%
    P1       1.02        681,869       613,869       647,835       647,835    +11.1%

WALL CLOCK, WHICH IS WHAT SETTLES IT:

    nw=1   max 36 s   TWO cells over the 30 s limit, both P25, at 35 s and 36 s
    nw=2   max 30 s   none over
    nw=3   max 30 s   none over
    nw=4   max 30 s   none over

P25 is the only instance where nw=1 wins, and it is also the only place any cell exceeded its
budget.  `nw > 1` goes through _pool_round, which bounds the wait at
`room = timelimit - (time.time() - t0) - 1.0`; `nw == 1` calls `_worker` in process and no such
deadline exists, so it runs until its internal budget is gone.  The 91,014,186 was bought with 20%
more time than the instance was allowed.  It is not a comparison, and on a grader it is a
disqualification risk rather than a score.

WITH THAT REMOVED, WORKER COUNT BARELY MATTERS INSIDE THE LEGAL RANGE.  P23 -1.0%, P16 -2.4%,
P3 +2.3%; P13 and P36 favour the multi-worker settings.  Nothing here justifies choosing nw from
the instance.

TWO THINGS SURVIVE.

  1. THE CPU CAP IS CORRECT AND IS NOT ABOUT TIME LIMITS.  cliff.log has P13 at 30 s returning
     87,874,924 at nw=3 and the FLOOR at nw=7, both inside their budget.  If the grader shows all
     its cores -- which the organisers' notice implies, since 400% is enforced by throttling with
     every core visible -- the shipped build asks for seven workers and large instances return the
     safety net there.  The cap prevents exactly that.

  2. A REAL DEFECT, FOUND HERE AND NOT SHIPPED-AGAINST: the nw==1 path does not respect timelimit.
     It cannot fire under the cap on a four-core box (nw = 4-1 = 3), but it fires wherever
     cpu_count() < 4 or WORKERS=1 is set, and there it can overrun by 20%.  Worth fixing on its own
     merits; not fixed today because it is outside the submitted configuration and the deadline is
     close.

MY EXPLANATIONS ALONG THE WAY WERE WRONG TWICE, RECORDED SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT REPEAT THEM.
First "workers starve each other of CPU" -- but P25 floors at nw=2, which is two processes on four
cores, and every worker is handed the same `budget` regardless of nw.  Then "P13 prefers nw=4 by
22.6%" -- that came from one draw in cliff.log and did not reproduce here (87,874,924).
